e whether to stick with B or shift to C. We should consider the arguments presented: Agent 3 (and 2) argue C is best despite communication issues. Agent 1 earlier argued B is best despite grumpiness. Now we need to respond, perhaps reconsider. We can either maintain B or shift to C. Need to provide evidence and reasoning.\n\nWe should consider the criteria: reliability, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility. Let's evaluate each candidate with our notes.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress very well (calmness), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but grumpy, uncooperative, makes nasty remarks, pretentious, wrong tone, weak memory for numbers (attention). So concerns: crew cooperation, professional communication, attention accuracy.\n\nCandidate C: makes correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (calmness), excellent attention skills (attention), understands complicated tech (technical), puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation), but difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So concerns: communication, egocentric, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but arrogant, weak leadership, moody, loner (crew cooperation, communication). No explicit stress resilience strong.\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership qualities (crew cooperation), but sometimes not good at taking criticism (feedback), can be unorganized (reliability), unfriendly, eats unhealthily, regarded as show\u2011off, not open to new ideas (cooperation, adaptability). So concerns: reliability, adaptability, communication.\n\nComparing B vs C: B has strong reliability and stress handling, but communication issues (grumpy, uncooperative). C has strong decision quality, calmness, attention, technical, crew concern, but communication difficulty and egocentric.\n\nWhich is more critical? Crew cooperation and communication are essential. Both have issues. B's uncooperative behavior may be more severe (makes nasty remarks, pretentious). C's difficulty communicating ideas may affect cockpit communication, but he puts concern for others above everything, which may mitigate. Egocentric may be a risk.\n\nGiven the panel's goal to choose best overall fit, we need to weigh. Perhaps we can shift to C, aligning with majority. Or we can argue B still better because reliability and stress handling are paramount for long-distance flights, and communication issues can be trained. But we need to provide reasoning.\n\nWe also need to bring in any missing evidence: maybe ask about Candidate A or D? But we need to keep contribution focused. We could ask for more info on Candidate A's reliability and stress resilience, but we need to give a recommendation now. The instruction: each turn must state a recommendation. So we must vote.\n\nGiven the majority now for C, we could shift to C, providing reasoning that C's strengths across most criteria outweigh B's communication concerns, and that C's crew concern is strong. We can also note that B's weak memory for numbers is a safety risk (attention). So we can change vote to C.\n\nThus we will vote for Candidate C. Provide argument referencing prior discussion: agree with Agent 3 that C's decision quality, calmness, attention, technical competence, and crew concern outweigh B's reliability and stress handling, especially given B's weak memory for numbers and uncooperative behavior.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C."}
